    {
      "id": "lpic1-command-permission-search",
      "version": 1,
      "track": "LPIC-1",
      "unit": "linux-command-location-diagnosis",
      "topic": "Command search and permissions",
      "title": "Diagnose a command skipped during search",
      "summary": "Investigate competing command files, restore one owner permission and preserve the fallback and audit tool.",
      "objective": "The fixed PATH searches /tools/preferred before /tools/fallback. reportctl selects the wrong copy; inspect why. Add only owner execute to /tools/preferred/reportctl; preserve its other permission bits. reportctl must select /tools/preferred/reportctl; auditctl must still select /tools/fallback/auditctl. Preserve all other modes, contents, owners, groups, folders and supplied settings.",
      "difficulty": "Intermediate",
      "minutes": 15,
      "steps": [
        "Understand the task",
        "Investigate the skipped file",
        "Verify lookup and preservation"
      ],
      "guide": [
        {
          "title": "Inspect both search results and metadata",
          "why": "Search order alone does not make a non-executable file eligible.",
          "action": "Use which and type -a -p for the supplied names. Inspect each path with ls -l before changing anything.",
          "commands": []
        },
        {
          "title": "Make one narrow repair",
          "why": "The owner can add execute without changing group or other permissions.",
          "action": "Repair the one owned preferred file. Retain the other original bits and all neighboring files.",
          "commands": []
        },
        {
          "title": "Verify the handover",
          "why": "The first selected path can be correct even after an excessive permission change.",
          "action": "Recheck both command names and all relevant permission letters, then choose Check work.",
          "commands": []
        }
      ],
      "explanation": "The preferred file originally had mode 0640: Alex could read/write it but could not execute it. Search therefore selected the executable fallback even though the preferred folder came first. Adding owner execute produces mode 0740 without widening group/other access. which then selects the preferred file; type -a -p still lists the preserved fallback second. auditctl is independent and stays in the fallback folder.",
      "hints": [
        "Run which reportctl and type -a -p reportctl, then inspect both named files with ls -l. Which copy is eligible now?",
        "Read the owner’s first three permission letters on /tools/preferred/reportctl. A readable file without owner x is skipped for Alex. Preserve the group and other bits.",
        "chmod u+x PATH adds only owner execute. Then repeat both reportctl searches and check auditctl; do not change PATH or disable the fallback."
      ],
      "sources": [
        "https://www.lpi.org/our-certifications/exam-101-102-objectives/ (LPIC-1 5.0, 104.7; checked 2026-11-05)",
        "https://www.gnu.org/software/bash/manual/html_node/Bash-Builtins.html (type -a -p; checked against installed Bash 5.3 manual 2026-11-05)",
        "https://www.gnu.org/software/coreutils/manual/html_node/Setting-Permissions.html (symbolic owner execute; installed GNU chmod manual checked 2026-11-05; web fetch unavailable)"
      ],
      "simplifications": "Bounded literal queries against supplied virtual regular files and fixed canonical PATH only. which prints the first executable match; type -a -p lists all matches. No artifact execution, aliases, builtins, functions, hash state, empty/relative PATH, whereis or arbitrary shell syntax. Only permissions on supplied caller-owned command files may change; directories, root-owned fallback, text and unrelated files stay fixed. Found/Missing are simulator results; exact utility stderr and Bash exit 126/127 are not reproduced.",
