      "terminal_teaching": {
        "prerequisite_note": "Build on file paths, owner/group/other permissions and ordered PATH. Type in the input below the terminal transcript; Run or Enter submits one simulated command. This practice uses different names and folders from the assignment.",
        "concepts": [
          {
            "title": "A name is searched in order",
            "text": "PATH is an ordered list of folders, separated by colons. A command name can exist in several folders. which NAME prints the first eligible executable file; type -a -p NAME lists all eligible external files in PATH order. The printed paths are search results, not a command run."
          },
          {
            "title": "Present is different from executable",
            "text": "A regular file can exist and be readable yet lack permission to execute. Search skips that file and may select a later copy. ls -l PATH shows its permission letters and owner. In -rw-r----- the owner has read/write, the group has read and nobody has execute. The x letter means execute for a file; on folders it means permission to search through them."
          },
          {
            "title": "Repair only what the task permits",
            "text": "Alex owns the preferred file. chmod u+x PATH adds execute for its owner while preserving every other permission bit. u means owner, + means add and x means execute. chmod u-x removes only that bit. A silent chmod is normal; inspect the file and repeat lookup to verify the change. Do not grant permissions to everyone or disable the fallback."
          },
          {
            "title": "Inspect without running a file",
            "text": "These supplied command files contain sample text. The simulated terminal supports which, type -a -p, ls and chmod for this activity; it does not run a found path or any file contents. The supplied PATH is fixed. Aliases, shell functions, command hashing, plain type and whereis are outside this activity."
          }
        ],
